  "query_preview": "forecast period as models suggest low pressure attempting \nto develop over portions of the western Atlantic. For now, a tight \npressure gradient is forecast to persist, keeping breezy to windy \nconditions potentially into mid next week, especially along the \ncoast. With a prolonged period of increased onshore flow, hazardous \ncoastal conditions are expected. Rough surf, large breaking waves, \nand a high risk of life-threatening rip currents will be present at \nall area beaches. Entering the water is highly discouraged! Long \nperiod onshore swells will contribute to beach erosion, and Moderate \nbeach erosion is likely, through multiple high tide cycles. \n\nDeep moisture persists areawide early Saturday before a drier \nairmass begins to filter southward behind the front. Greatest rain \nchances Saturday are forecast south of Orlando and the Cape (50-70%) \nwith earlier advection of drier air keeping isolated to scattered \ncoverage across northern areas. Deeper moisture may linger in \nvicinity of the Treasure Coast and Okeechobee county into early next \nweek as the front stalls. This should keep the greatest chances \nfor scattered to locally numerous showers and a few storms across \nthe south, but moist onshore flow should still allow for rounds of\nonshore moving showers along much of the coast and through the \nI-95 corridor into next week. Persistent bands of showers and any \nstorms could lead to locally heavy to excessive rainfall, \nproducing flooding issues, mostly across the Treasure Coast. A \nMarginal Risk for excessive rainfall (Level 1 of 4) exists across \nthis portion of east central Florida this weekend. \n\nA cooler airmass spreads east central Florida this weekend with \nhighs ranging the the mid 70s to low 80s by Sunday. Some gradual \nwarming is then forecast through the middle of next week, but \ntemperatures mostly in the 80s remain near to below normal.",
